issue.body:
Hi

I recently discovered what seems to be a bug in the latest silex version. It seems that sessions cannot be invalidated with `$app['session']->invalidate()` without an explicit `$app['session']->start()` in e.g. the `before` middleware.

It seems that sessions should be started automatically upon initial access (#274). I've created a quick sample app to demonstrate the problem ([https://github.com/peschee/silex-session-bug](https://github.com/peschee/silex-session-bug)) which has the following structure:

``` php
<?php

require __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';

use Silex\Application;
use Silex\Provider\SessionServiceProvider;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

define('SESSION_KEY', 'some_key');

$app = new Application();

$app->register(new SessionServiceProvider());

$app->before(function (Request $request) use ($app) {
//    $app['session']->start();
});

$app->get('/', function () use ($app) {
    return sprintf('Main<br /><br />Session: %s', $app['session']->get(SESSION_KEY));
});

$app->get('/set', function () use ($app) {
    $app['session']->set(SESSION_KEY, 'some_value');

    return sprintf('Set<br /><br />Session: %s', $app['session']->get(SESSION_KEY));
});

$app->get('/invalidate', function () use ($app) {
    $app['session']->invalidate();

    return sprintf('Set<br /><br />Session: %s', $app['session']->get(SESSION_KEY));
});

$app->run();
```

The `/set` route correctly sets the session value. However, if I visis `/` after having invalidated the session in `/invalidate`, the stored session value is still there. Adding `$app['session']->start()` in the before middleware solves the problem.

Is this expected behavior? If so, we need to change the docs accordingly.

This fix is about   6a15a3a 
Also refer to: https://github.com/symfony/symfony/issues/7380

This fix is not valid because it generate another bug if _SESSION is not set! ($_SESSION is not defined)

I've modified code to this:

```
   $ret = session_regenerate_id($destroy);

    // workaround for https://bugs.php.net/bug.php?id=61470 as suggested by David Grudl
    session_write_close();
   $sessionSet = isset($_SESSION);
    if($sessionSet) {
        $backup = $_SESSION;
    }
    session_start();
    if($sessionSet) {
        $_SESSION = $backup;
    }
```

Please fix it.

The case happened for me when having no cookie/ PHPSESSID, using FOSUserBundle on http://BASE_URL/register/confirm/TOKEN

Thanks
